  "derivation": "Model: R ~ N(0,tau^2), eps ~ N(0,sigma^2) independent, S=R+eps, v=Var(eps)=sigma^2, w=Var(R)=tau^2, m=E[R|S=s]=rho s with rho=w/(w+v)=tau^2/(tau^2+sigma^2), u=Var(R|S=s)=w v/(w+v)=rho v. Y=aR-k|a|, so E[Y|S=s]=a m-k|a| and Var(Y|S=s)=a^2 u (k|a| deterministic). Objective J(a|s)=a m-k|a|-(lambda/2) a^2 u. Set c=lambda*u/2. For a>=0: J=a(m-k)-c a^2 (up to cap 1); for a<=0: J=a(m+k)-c a^2 (up to cap -1). Exact optimizer: if m<=k, max over a>=0 is at a=0 giving 0 (the positive branch is nonpositive since m-k<=0 and -c a^2<=0); if m>=-k, max over a<=0 is at a=0 giving 0. Therefore if |m|<=k the optimizer is exactly a*=0 and J*=0 (no-trade region; also includes lambda arbitrary since at a=0 the risk term vanishes; if lambda=0 the same region logic applies but with linear objective: a*=1 when m>k, a*=-1 when m<k, 0 when |m|<=k, ties any a in [0,1] or [-1,0] respectively give same value m-k or -(m+k)). If m>k: candidate a0=(m-k)/(lambda u) (if lambda>0), a*=clip(a0,0,1); if lambda=0 then a*=1. If m<k: symmetric a0=(m+k)/(lambda u), a*=clip(a0,0,1). This covers ties at m=±k (a*=0). Since the objective on each sign region is a concave quadratic (lambda>0) or linear (lambda=0), and the two regions meet at a=0 where the objective is 0 and continuous (the absolute value makes J continuous and piecewise smooth; actually strictly continuous with a kink possible at a=0 only in derivative), each branch's global max on its interval is the candidate given, and the overall global max is the larger of the two branch maxima and 0 at a=0; since at most one branch is active (sign of m matches sign of m-k or m+k given |m|>k), the global maximizer is as stated, and it is globally optimal because J is continuous and each interval's restriction is concave/unimodal, so no other point beats it. J* formula: if |m|<=k: 0. If m>k: if a0<=1 then J*=(m-k)^2/(2 lambda u) (lambda>0), else m-k-c. If m<k similarly with |m|+k (note for negative m: let h=-m>0, candidate a=-clip((h-k)/(lambda u),0,1), J*=(h-k)^2/(2 lambda u) if interior else h-k-c). Interpretation: m is the conditional EXPECTED return given the signal, not the realized R (which remains random with variance u around m), and even a positive m-k or positive J only holds under the assumed Gaussian model; it says nothing about real markets or whether the model is valid. Positive conditional expected net return E[Y|S=s]>0 is not the same as positive realized return Y (Y can be negative), and neither implies the Gaussian assumption is true.",
